, 0.023162841796875, 0.01165771484375, -0.0136566162109375, -0.0110321044921875, 0.016998291015625, 0.014892578125, 0.00897216796875, 0.032928466796875, -0.005100250244140625, 0.0097198486328125, -0.018463134765625, 0.0194549560546875, 0.024871826171875, 0.03594970703125, -0.005901336669921875, 0.01209259033203125, -0.0244140625, -0.017425537109375, 0.026275634765625, -0.007415771484375, 0.0206146240234375, -0.0016632080078125, -0.045501708984375, -0.0207366943359375, 0.014129638671875, -0.001369476318359375, 0.0197296142578125, 0.031494140625, -0.0142059326171875, -0.01074981689453125, -0.010894775390625, 0.00890350341796875, 0.003978729248046875, -0.01160430908203125, 0.005405426025390625, -0.02001953125, -0.0266265869140625, -0.007049560546875, -0.0278167724609375, 0.0007495880126953125, -0.00759124755859375, 0.0001291036605834961, 0.0285186767578125, -0.019500732421875, -0.00669097900390625, 0.0049591064453125, 0.017974853515625, 0.00727081298828125, -0.043670654296875, -0.002063751220703125, 0.0048828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) raises important questions about governance and control: Who sets the direction and at what stage in the process? How much autonomy should AI systems be given? This article investigates when, how, and through what mechanisms humans guide the work done by AI\u2014and how these patterns change based on the way people interact with AI systems.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\nTo clarify terms, we distinguish between two modes of delegation:\n\n- **Specified delegation:** Humans set explicit goals or constraints for the AI before it begins working, such as providing step-by-step instructions or detailed requirements up front.  \n  *Example: A user programs the AI to summarize a document, specifying word limits and key topics to include.*\n\n- **Iterative coproduction:** Humans collaborate with the AI during the creation process, adjusting, revising, or steering outputs as they are generated.  \n  *Example: A user interacts in a chat session, giving feedback after each draft, gradually refining the output.*\n\n*Delegation* refers broadly to any assignment of cognitive steps from a human to a machine. *Prior human direction* refers specifically to the extent to which human intent is built into the task before the AI begins executing it.\n\n**Research Questions**\n\nThis study asks:  \n1. At what points are humans most influential in shaping AI output when delegating tasks?\n2. How do these patterns differ between structured interfaces and more interactive environments?\n\n**Approach and Data**\n\nWe use the Anthropic Economic Index (**AEI**), a dataset that tracks aggregated human-AI task interactions, for April and May 2026. AEI data allow us to analyze at scale how delegation unfolds. We compare two main AI interaction modes:\n\n- **1P API:** Direct, technical access to Anthropic\u2019s API, commonly used by developers or for structured processes.\n- **Claude.ai:** Usage via graphical interfaces (e.g., Chat, Cowork), typically involving broader, less formal interactions.\n\nThese modes were chosen because they capture distinct user types\u2014developers versus general users\u2014and different styles of engagement with AI.\n\n**Methodology**\n\nTo investigate how \"work-related orientation\" affects delegation, we simulate a shift in user intent by reallocating 10 percentage points from personal to work-related use within each eligible usage record (an \u201celigible record\u201d is a unit of observation with enough data on both personal and work-related usages). This stepwise reallocation enables us to estimate how changing the context from personal to professional influences delegation profiles, controlling for task and user characteristics.\n\nWe analyze data grouped by \"node-month pairs\"\u2014unique combinations of specific occupational task categories (based on the O*NET taxonomy, which organizes work activities) and calendar months. For statistical analysis, we focus on \"level-0 O*NET tasks\" (the most basic unit of task categorization) to ensure comparability. In examining iterative processes, \"common support\" means we restrict our sample to node-month pairs present in both interaction modes (1,411 pairs), ensuring fair comparisons.\n\n**Findings and Interpretation**\n\n- When AI use becomes more work-oriented (simulated by the 10-point reallocation), **specified delegation** rises by 2.76 points (on a standardized scale; 95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) in the 1P API mode and 1.45 points (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) in Claude.ai. Here, each \"point\" represents a one-step increase on our normalized delegation scale. These moderate but meaningful increases indicate that, particularly through APIs, professional tasks foster more up-front human direction\u2014users encode more instructions and requirements beforehand.\n- For **iterative coproduction**, the profile shifts slightly downward in the 1P API mode (-0.30 points) but upward in Claude.ai (+0.15 points), with a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). This suggests that interactive environments like Claude.ai promote more ongoing human intervention and revision, even if the effect is not large.\n\nIn substantive terms, these effect sizes suggest that in professional settings, especially when using structured APIs, users tend to formalize instructions and let AI proceed autonomously, whereas interactive environments promote continued human oversight and adjustment.\n\n**Limitations and Boundary Conditions**\n\nThere are several caveats to our analysis. The AEI may not capture all user contexts, and simulation of work-related reallocation, although common to causal inference, is a proxy for changes that may not fully reflect real-world behavior. Unobserved confounders\u2014such as varying task complexity across modes, or differences in user expertise\u2014may also influence the results. The findings are particularly relevant for generative AI in task-based knowledge work; they may not generalize to other domains or AI types.\n\n**Implications and Future Directions**\n\nOur results have implications for designing AI systems and workplace policies: understanding when human intent is most embedded can inform guidelines for oversight, responsibility, and safety in delegating cognitive tasks. UIs promoting collaborative, in-process revision may suit tasks needing adaptability, while APIs may encourage decisive, up-front planning. Future research should examine delegation dynamics in other sectors, explore the circumstances under which iterative human involvement adds significant value, and assess outcomes beyond task completion\u2014such as creativity, satisfaction, and error rates.\n\nBy clarifying *when* and *how* human guidance leaves its imprint on AI-mediated work, this analysis advances debates beyond the extent of automation. It shows that the temporal pattern of human input\u2014shaped by context and interface\u2014remains a central factor in realizing the potential and managing the risks of generative AI."}}